// microprogram.ctxdsl — hand-authored microcode listing for a tiny
// driver that talks to the handshake_peripheral SV module.
//
// The microcode is modelled as a 4-state CTXDSL automaton:
//   - WaitForReady: initial state, the controller is polling
//   - AssertReq:    raise req to the peripheral
//   - PollAck:      wait for the peripheral to enter ACTIVE
//   - Done:         the handshake completed
//
// Labels:
//   - tick_microcode is the microprogram's internal step event
//     (silicon clock tick from the microcode's point of view).
//   - The handshake_peripheral SV adapter emits its own
//     port-binding labels (`req_T`, `req_F`, etc.) which compose
//     asynchronously with this microprogram per the verify
//     framework's direct alphabet binding.
//
// The whole point of this example is to demonstrate the framework's
// adapter-agnosticism: a hand-authored CTXDSL source + an SV-
// extracted source compose without code changes on the framework
// side. This is a "framework demonstration" pairing rather than a
// verification target — the two automata share no labels in this
// minimal form, so the asynchronous composition is just an
// interleaving of their independent state spaces.

context Microprogram {
    alphabet {
        label tick_microcode;
    }

    automata {
        automaton Microprogram {
            states {
                state WaitForReady initial;
                state AssertReq;
                state PollAck;
                state Done;
            }
            transitions {
                transition WaitForReady -> AssertReq on label tick_microcode;
                transition AssertReq -> PollAck on label tick_microcode;
                transition PollAck -> Done on label tick_microcode;
                transition Done -> WaitForReady on label tick_microcode;
            }
        }
    }
}
